# Global contact opening with frozen diameter endpoints

**Date:** 2026-10-11  
**Base:** `ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc`, state `2026-10-10.audit.234`  
**Target:** independent Level I global source competitor route  
**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting promotion  
**Level I:** STILL OPEN. The general contact-opening bridge in Section 7 is unproved.

Every construction below retains the full source, including arbitrary contact-isolated
points. No second configuration is silently substituted for the source. The only
load-bearing canonical input is normalized nonnegative KKT equilibrium, shared
theory `01-local-structure.md`, Theorem 4.1. Elementary convex-hull, circle, and
lattice estimates are proved or specified below. No ordinary bar-rigidity or
contact-minimality assumption is used.

## 1. The direct global contradiction

```text
finite full-source contact opening, fixing all actual diameter endpoints
  -> another n-point global minimizer with the same actual diameter graph
  -> normalized KKT equilibrium at that endpoint
  -> some contact component has diameter at least (sqrt(3)-1)D
```

For the grid charts below the motion leaves only contact components of diameter
O(D^(2/3)), giving a contradiction. The endpoint is actually constructed; all
inactive lower and upper pairs are retained using their finite strict margins.

## 2. Balanced diameter-force components must be macroscopic

**Lemma.** In any finite planar unit-separated source of diameter D having
normalized nonnegative KKT equilibrium, some component of the positive-contact
graph has diameter at least `(sqrt(3)-1)D`. More precisely, this holds for every
positive-contact component incident to a positive diameter multiplier.

**Proof.** Let U be such a component and let r=diam(U). If a positive diameter
edge has both endpoints in U, r>=D and the assertion follows. Otherwise sum
equilibrium over U. Positive contact forces cancel internally, leaving

\[
\sum_{e\in\partial_H U}\beta_e(a_e-b_e)=0,
\]

where a_e is in U and b_e is outside U. The sum of these beta weights is positive.
After normalizing them to probabilities, the two endpoint distributions have the
same centroid c. Their variances satisfy

\[
V_a\le r^2/3,\qquad V_b\le D^2/3.
\]

Here the elementary planar Jung bound puts a diameter-d set in a disk of radius
at most d/sqrt(3), and variance is no larger than the mean squared distance from
any chosen center. To recall the Jung proof: a minimum enclosing circle is
supported by two antipodal points or by three points whose convex hull contains
its center. In the latter case their largest central gap is between 2pi/3 and pi,
so one chord is at least sqrt(3) times the radius; the two-point case is stronger.

Since every crossing edge has length D, Cauchy-Schwarz for the joint endpoint
distribution gives

\[
D^2=\mathbb E|a-b|^2
\le(\sqrt{V_a}+\sqrt{V_b})^2
\le(r+D)^2/3.
\]

Thus r>=(sqrt(3)-1)D. There is at least one positive diameter multiplier because
their normalized sum is one. Its endpoint belongs to a positive-contact component:
otherwise the same argument for its singleton gives an immediate contradiction.
This proves existence as well as the componentwise statement. Zero-weight contacts
do not affect the argument. The same size lower bound also applies to the full
contact component containing each such positive component.

### A universal necessary theorem for every global minimizer

**Frozen-island theorem (proved candidate awaiting promotion).** For any finite
global minimizer, let Q be its complete set of actual diameter endpoints and define

\[
F_Q=\{w:r_{ij}\cdot(w_i-w_j)=0\ (ij\in C),\quad w_i=0\ (i\in Q)\}.
\]

Call a contact frozen if w_i=w_j for every w in F_Q. The graph of frozen contacts
has a connected component of diameter at least (sqrt(3)-1)D which meets Q.
Every vertex of that component is fixed pointwise by every velocity in F_Q.
This theorem uses neither a lattice nor a triangle-free hypothesis.

**Proof.** For each nonfrozen contact, the subspace where w_i=w_j is proper in
F_Q. A finite union of such proper subspaces cannot cover F_Q. Choose one w
outside all of them, or w=0 if all contacts are frozen. For a small finite t,
the straight-line motion p_i+t w_i opens every nonfrozen contact by its positive
t^2|w_i-w_j|^2 term and preserves every frozen contact. It fixes every actual
diameter pair, and the finite strict margins preserve all old inactive lower
and upper pairs, including contact-isolate pairs. Its contact graph is exactly
the frozen-contact graph. Global minimality makes this feasible endpoint another
global minimizer. Apply normalized KKT equilibrium there and the extent lemma
above. A positive-contact component incident to a positive diameter edge is
macroscopic and meets Q; its full frozen-contact component is also macroscopic.
Along every frozen edge the chosen velocity is equal at its two endpoints, so
each frozen component translates rigidly. The one meeting Q has zero velocity
at that pin and is therefore unchanged. For *every* vector in F_Q, equality
along all frozen edges propagates that zero pin velocity to the whole component.
The claimed component and its size thus belong to the original source.

This is a spatial strengthening of the usual adapted-stress argument: it forces
a macroscopic pointwise frozen island instead of assuming that most source
vertices are stressed. It does not imply that the island contains an exact unit
triangle, has quadratic population, or is rigid when the diameter pins are freed.

## 3. An explicit full-source contact-opening chart

Suppose every nontrivial component G_j of the **full actual unit-contact graph**
uses at most two independent unit directions u_j,v_j. Assume their basis angle is
in [pi/3,2pi/3]. A one-direction component may use any perpendicular second
direction. Connectivity labels every point in G_j uniquely as

\[
p_{ab}=z_j+a u_j+b v_j,\qquad (a,b)\in\mathbb Z^2.
\]

This follows by summing the integer unit steps along a contact path; independence
of the basis makes the resulting coordinates consistent around every cycle.
Different components can have different directions, origins, and label sets.
No full-product, convexity, common lattice, or common phase assumption is imposed.

Let P_j be the actual diameter endpoints lying in G_j, and put q_j=|P_j|. Let
A_j and B_j be the sets of their integer a- and b-coordinates. Choose functions
f_j and g_j on the finitely many source coordinates satisfying:

- f_j=0 on B_j, and its values elsewhere are nonzero and pairwise distinct;
- g_j=0 on A_j, and its values elsewhere are nonzero and pairwise distinct.

For every source point in G_j choose its velocity uniquely by

\[
u_j\cdot w_{ab}=f_j(b),\qquad v_j\cdot w_{ab}=g_j(a).
\]

Give every original contact-isolated point velocity zero. All actual diameter
endpoints have velocity zero, including any isolated endpoints.

For a horizontal unit contact the relative velocity has zero u_j projection.
It is zero exactly when both adjacent a-coordinates belong to A_j; otherwise it
is nonzero. Vertical contacts have the corresponding rule for B_j. Consequently
every old contact either remains exactly unchanged or obeys

\[
|r+t\Delta w|^2=1+t^2|\Delta w|^2>1\quad(t\ne0).
\]

All old noncontact pairs have strictly positive lower margin. Every old pair
outside the diameter graph has strictly positive upper margin. There are finitely
many full-source pairs, so one common sufficiently small positive t preserves
every one of these margins. All old diameter pairs are fixed exactly. The new
source therefore has the same cardinality, diameter D, and actual diameter graph;
its contacts are precisely the old contacts with zero relative velocity.
This check includes pairs between different original components and pairs
involving all old contact isolates. No new contacts are introduced.

Every new contact component is translated rigidly by this motion. Its a-range
can cross an integer gap only when both endpoints of that gap are in A_j.
Its b-range has the analogous restriction. Thus its old and new diameters obey

\[
r_{\rm new}\le (|A_j|-1)_+ + (|B_j|-1)_+
\le 2(q_j-1)_+.
\]

If q_j=0 or 1, every old contact in G_j opens and the remaining components are
singletons. This is an exact finite deformation, not a formal rank argument.

## 4. A uniform loaded-lattice hull bound

Every actual diameter endpoint is a vertex of the full source hull. Indeed, if p
were a nontrivial convex combination of other source points and |p-y|=D,
strict convexity of squared distance would give |p-y|^2<D^2. Hence every point
of P_j is a vertex of conv(P_j).

The unit lattice with basis angle in [pi/3,2pi/3] is uniformly unit-separated:
for a nonzero integer vector (a,b),

\[
|a u_j+b v_j|^2\ge a^2+b^2-|ab|\ge1.
\]

If q_j>=64, let L=sqrt(q_j)/8>=1. At most 4(L+1/2)^2<=9L^2 lattice vectors
have norm below L, by placing disjoint radius-1/2 disks around them. The oriented
edge vectors of the convex polygon conv(P_j) are distinct. At least q_j/2 of
its sides therefore have length at least L, because 9L^2=9q_j/64<q_j/2.
Writing P for its perimeter,

\[
P\ge q_j^{3/2}/16,
\qquad q_j\le(16P)^{2/3}\le(16\pi D)^{2/3}.
\]

The final inequality is Cauchy's perimeter formula
`P=integral_0^pi width(phi) dphi<=pi D`. Collinear pin sets contain at most two
points because all of their points must be extreme. The small-q case gives the
uniform estimate

\[
q_j\le64+(16\pi D)^{2/3}\le64+16D^{2/3}.
\]

The last inequality uses pi<4. This is an elementary lattice-hull argument; no
angle-dependent Jarnik constant or outside uniformity assumption is imported.

## 5. Global theorem without ordinary bar rigidity

**Theorem (proved candidate awaiting promotion).** If D>=2^24, no planar
unit-separated global diameter minimizer can have every contact component in
the two-direction chart class of Section 3. This includes arbitrarily many
components, arbitrary phases, arbitrary contact-isolated points, holes, and
angles that approach pi/3 or 2pi/3 at any rate.

**Proof.** If the source were global, the full-source motion in Section 3 would
produce another global minimizer of the same cardinality and diameter. By
Sections 3--4 each of its full contact components has diameter at most

\[
128+32D^{2/3}\le128+D/8<D/4,
\]

where D>=2^24 implies D^(1/3)>=256. KKT equilibrium at this actual new source
is therefore impossible by Section 2, whose required component diameter is
(sqrt(3)-1)D>D/4. This contradicts global minimality. The proof uses no ordinary
active-bar rigidity and does not need the positive-contact core to contain a
large fraction of the source.

The D threshold is deliberately crude. Since disjoint radius-1/2 source disks
fit in a disk of radius D+1/2 centered at any source point,
`n<=4(D+1/2)^2`. Thus the theorem also applies at every sufficiently large n.

## 6. Whole unit-rhombic-lattice corollary, including hexagonal endpoints

**Corollary.** Every sufficiently large global minimizer contained in one
translate and rotation of a lattice generated by two unit vectors of angle
in [pi/3,2pi/3] contains a unit equilateral triangle. The angle may vary with n.
No source rigidity, full-product shape, or absence-of-isolates hypothesis is
required.

For an angle strictly between the endpoints the only unit lattice differences
are the two generator axes, so Section 5 rules out a global minimizer in that
lattice, triangle-free or otherwise. This is a uniform exclusion of the whole
strict-oblique source class, including the inverse-diameter shear regime.

At either endpoint the lattice is triangular. A triangle-free subset has a
fixed positive density deficit. Here is the complete counting argument. Put
u=(1,0), v=(1/2,sqrt(3)/2), a0=sqrt(3)/2. The index-three sublattice generated
by 2u-v and u+v partitions the unit lattice into disjoint unit triples
`{z,z+u,z+v}`. A triangle-free source occupies at most two sites in each triple.
If a triple meets K=conv(A), its root z lies in K+B(0,1). The centered fundamental
parallelogram at that root has area 3a0 and radius at most 3/2. These disjoint
cells all lie in K+B(0,5/2). Thus

\[
n\le\frac{2}{3a_0}
 \left(\operatorname{area}K+\tfrac52\operatorname{per}K+
                 \tfrac{25}{4}\pi\right)
\le\frac{\pi D^2}{6a_0}+O(D).
\]

The parallel-body formula and isodiametric inequality are used with the actual
source hull. Conversely translated triangular-lattice averaging gives every
global minimizer `D^2<=4a0 n/pi`, or `n>=pi D^2/(4a0)`. To recall the averaging
argument, a disk of area a0 n has mean translated lattice population n, so one
translate contains at least n sites; those sites have diameter at most
sqrt(4a0 n/pi). The quadratic leading coefficients in the two population
inequalities differ, so the triangle-free endpoint case is excluded for all
sufficiently large D. This proves the corollary.

The existing literature audit of Das, Theorem 1.5, retains an ordinary-rigidity
premise. The present proof removes it in this whole unit-rhombic-lattice scope.
The canonical finite oblique comparison already excludes angles sufficiently
far from the hexagonal endpoints; the new finite contact opening also excludes
angles arbitrarily close to them. These statements remain candidates until
independently audited and promoted.

## 7. Exact remaining Level I bridge

The following **global contact-opening bridge is unproved**:

> Every sufficiently large triangle-free global minimizer admits a finite
> full-source motion fixing all actual diameter endpoints, preserving every
> old noncontact lower margin and every inactive upper margin, and leaving
> only contact components of diameter less than (sqrt(3)-1)D.

Section 2 would contradict that endpoint's KKT equilibrium and settle Level I.
Sections 3--5 establish the bridge only for the explicitly defined two-direction
component class. A general contact component can use many directions; assigning
two-coordinate row/column velocities there is not justified. Phase labels or
virtual train charts cannot be substituted for an actual source contact chart.
No general crystallization or chart-extraction theorem is asserted.
A full Level I proof may use an unrestricted same-cardinality replacement that
moves the old diameter endpoints. The frozen-endpoint bridge is a sufficient
research target, not a necessary restriction on the problem's solution.

## 8. Exact verification and lifecycle

`research/verify_global_loaded_grid_opening_2026_10_11.py` checks the full-pair
motion on a 2x3 square product with retained unit pairs, a 7x7 square source
with nine removed grid labels and one arbitrary rational isolate, and the
nine-label rational oblique packet from the companion report. It checks all
old/new contacts, all actual diameter pairs, every full-source lower and upper
constraint, and the component range bound. The 2x2 square is a binding control:
its loaded-coordinate motion is zero and its contact component remains of
diameter D, as required by its known optimality.

The script also verifies the index-three triangular-lattice partition exactly.
Finite output does not prove the general deformation theorem, the lattice-hull
bound, the asymptotic counting argument, or global optimality. Those arguments
are analytical above. All claims are unreviewed candidates awaiting an
independent audit. No canonical state or active route prompt is changed.
